@node introduction "Introduction"
Back when version of 41.0 of ixemul.library was released, it came with
numerous new configuration options, one of which I wanted to use.  By playing
with ixconfig, the configuration program that accompanied that version of the
library, I saw that this program had two deficiencies:

  a. It had almost as many command line options as UNIX ls, which are hard to
     remember.
  b. Making the new configuration persistent was done using a horrible kludge,
     namely the -s option of ixconfig, which instructed ixconfig never to
     return, to make ixemul.library unflushable.  Even if one had memory to
     burn, permanently allocating 120+ Kbytes, just to remember the handful of
     bits that define the configuration of ixemul.library, was a terrible
     waste.

Having a bit of spare time, I decided to try addressing these two points: By
writing a graphical configuration program, I believe I addressed the first,
and by not supporting the equivalent of the -s option, I hoped to give the
maintainers of ixemul.library the incentive to read, at library startup, the
configuration from a file similar to the one that ixprefs maintains, so that
ixemul.library would not have to stay permanently in memory if it is to
remember its configuration.

Starting with ixemul.library version 42.0 this functionality has been
implemented.

For ixemul.library 80.3, ixprefs still identifies itself as v2.8.
The configuration window now exposes three distinct I/O settings:
block size, physical blocks and a restricted 1x/2x multiplier.
See @{"What changed in 80.3" link new803} and the
@{"preferences format" link format803} chapter for the new encoding.
A separate @{"ixprefscheck" link check803} utility can inspect and
convert preference files without altering live library settings.
@endnode
@node new803 "What's new for ixemul.library 80.3"
The November 2026 ixprefs source update keeps the familiar program
version 2.8 and most existing controls, but separates the preferred
I/O configuration into three values:

  Block size (bytes)                   default 512
  Physical blocks per logical block  default 64
  Extra I/O multiplier                default 1x (choices 1x, 2x)

These are combined using the formula explained under
@{"I/O settings and examples" link io_settings}.  The maximum
combined size is 512 KiB (524288 bytes).

The window also has a @{"Reported block size" link reportedblock}
button to query the current AmigaDOS volume.  It is diagnostic only.

The original 28-byte binary settings layout is retained.  For 80.3
records, the previously obsolete red-zone field holds the block size
and the factor field has a tagged representation of physical blocks
and the 1x/2x multiplier.  See @{"File format" link format803}.

Older preferences with an ordinary positive factor can still be
interpreted by the new library.  However, older libraries cannot
recognize the tagged 80.3 factor.  Before downgrading, see
@{"80.2 compatibility" link compat803}.

The new @{"ixprefscheck" link check803} command can inspect and
convert files without opening the ixprefs GUI.  It never changes
live library settings.  It is separate from the ixprefs CLI.
@endnode

@node io_settings "I/O block size, physical blocks, multiplier"
The I/O settings describe a preferred logical I/O size used in
ixemul's file metadata and buffering decisions.  They do NOT change
AmigaDOS disk geometry, partition allocation or filesystem blocks.

The calculation is:

  Preferred I/O size = Block size * Physical blocks * Multiplier

Examples:

  512 * 64 * 1 =  32768 bytes =  32 KiB (default)
  512 * 64 * 2 =  65536 bytes =  64 KiB
 4096 * 64 * 2 = 524288 bytes = 512 KiB (upper limit)
 4096 * 65 * 2 = 532480 bytes: INVALID, exceeds 512 KiB

Values must satisfy all of these conditions:

  Block size:       positive, no larger than 524288
  Physical blocks: positive, no larger than 65535
  Multiplier:      exactly 1 or 2
  Product:         no larger than 524288 bytes

The allowed number of physical blocks therefore depends on the other
settings.  ixprefs rejects an invalid combination on Use or Save.
The current ix_set_settings() also checks the combination and falls
back to 512 * 64 * 1 if the block settings are invalid.  This does
not mean that all unrelated preferences fields are fully validated.

Higher I/O sizes are NOT automatically faster.  Choose values after
measurement; the default is a conservative starting point.

The GUI gadgets and their CLI equivalents are:
  @{"Block size" link blocksize}          -B / --block-size
  @{"Physical blocks" link blocks}       -b / --fs-buf-factor
  @{"I/O multiplier" link iomultiplier}    -X / --io-multiplier
@endnode

@node blocksize "Block size (bytes)"
The number of bytes in one configured basic I/O block.  The default
for the new 80.3 sources is 512 bytes, including for ordinary old
preferences whose unused red-zone field is zero.

Unlike the physical block count, this value was not independently
editable in the original ixprefs GUI.  For 80.3, it is stored in the
28-byte record's offset 16 field, previously red_zone_size.

The CLI option is -B N or --block-size N.  The reported volume
size shown by @{"Reported block size" link reportedblock} may help
with diagnosis, but it is not copied into this setting automatically.
See @{"I/O settings" link io_settings} for the 512 KiB limit.
@endnode

@node iomultiplier "Extra I/O multiplier (1x or 2x)"
The cycle gadget is labelled "Extra I/O multiplier" in the current
C GUI source.  It controls an extra factor of exactly 1 or 2.
It is sometimes described as the "I/O buffer multiplier".

For example, 64 physical blocks at 512 bytes become 32 KiB at 1x
and 64 KiB at 2x.  The multiplier is independent of the physical
block count in the 80.3 tagged preferences format.

The CLI option is -X N or --io-multiplier N, where N is 1 or 2.
The 512 KiB combined limit applies to both settings.
@endnode

@node reportedblock "Reported block size (diagnostic)"
Clicking "Reported block size" queries AmigaDOS Info() for the
volume containing the program's current directory, and displays its
reported id_BytesPerBlock value.  The operation is read-only.

It is NOT a button to set the configured block size.  The reported
number is not necessarily the best logical I/O size for ixemul.
Values outside the typical 512-4096 byte range prompt a warning to
verify the filesystem or handler's report.
@endnode

@node files803 "The active and persistent preferences files"
The preferences filename is ixemul.prefs.

  ENV:ixemul.prefs     Active environment preferences
  ENVARC:ixemul.prefs  Persistent preferences for future sessions

At library initialization, ixemul uses AmigaDOS GetVar() with binary
variable handling to read the settings.  If it cannot obtain the
expected 28-byte record, its intended fallback is the library's
default settings.  A missing file should not by itself require
creating a dummy preferences file.

When started normally, ixprefs calls ix_get_settings() and displays
values already held by ixemul.library.  It does not directly read
ENVARC:ixemul.prefs just to open the GUI.

Use writes ENV:ixemul.prefs; Save writes ENV:ixemul.prefs and then
ENVARC:ixemul.prefs; Last Saved reads ENVARC:ixemul.prefs.  A
failure to access ENVARC: therefore affects persistence and Last
Saved, but does not alone establish the cause of a GUI-start crash.

Some Amiga-compatible systems use ENV: handlers instead of a copy
of ENVARC: in RAM:ENV.  Inspect the actual assignments and handler
behaviour on the system concerned.  Do not assume ENV: and ENVARC:
are the same directory or that their contents are synchronized.

To inspect either existing file WITHOUT changing library settings:

  ixprefscheck ENV:ixemul.prefs
  ixprefscheck ENVARC:ixemul.prefs

See @{"Troubleshooting" link troubles803} for relevant cautions.
@endnode

@node format803 "The 28-byte ixemul.prefs binary format"
The file contains a fixed-size struct ix_settings record: seven
consecutive 32-bit values, stored in big-endian order on m68k.
The 80.3 revision uses the SAME 28-byte layout as older versions.
It changes the meaning of two existing fields; it does not append
a header or increase the file size.

 Byte    Size  Field                 Meaning
 ------  ----  --------------------  -------------------------------
  0..3     4   version               Library major version (80)
  4..7     4   revision              Library revision (2 or 3)
  8..11    4   flags                 Unsigned ixemul option bits
 12..15    4   membuf_limit          MEMBUF maximum, in bytes
 16..19    4   red_zone_size /       Old: obsolete red-zone field
              fs_blocksize          80.3: basic I/O block size
 20..23    4   fs_buf_factor         Old: plain physical count
                                     80.3: possibly tagged
 24..27    4   network_type          0=Auto, 1=None, 2=AS225,
                                     3=AmiTCP

All fields except flags are stored as signed 32-bit values in the
original C structure.  The 80.3 factor marker remains positive in
a signed 32-bit integer, but must not be mistaken for a block count.

LEGACY RECORD (e.g. version=80, revision=2):
  fs_buf_factor = 64        means 64 physical blocks
  old red_zone_size = 0     yields the default 512-byte block size
  multiplier = 1x           no separate field in legacy format

80.3 TAGGED RECORD (version=80, revision=3):
  fs_blocksize = 512        stored at byte offset 16
  fs_buf_factor = tagged    stored at byte offset 20

The tagged factor is formed as follows:

  Factor = 0x4D000000 | (2x ? 0x00010000 : 0) | blocks

  Bits 31..17: marker recognized by mask 0xFFFE0000
  Bit 16:      clear = 1x, set = 2x
  Bits 15..0:  unsigned physical block count (1..65535)

  64 physical blocks, 1x -> 0x4D000040 (1291845696 decimal)
  64 physical blocks, 2x -> 0x4D010040 (1291911232 decimal)

A complete example record (80.3, defaults, 1x) consists of these
seven big-endian four-byte words, in on-disk order:

  00 00 00 50  version = 80
  00 00 00 03  revision = 3
  00 00 0C 01  flags = 0x00000C01
  00 00 00 00  MEMBUF = 0
  00 00 02 00  block size = 512
  4D 00 00 40  tagged factor = 64 blocks at 1x
  00 00 00 00  network = Auto

Changing only the sixth word to 4D 01 00 40 gives 2x and a total
preferred I/O size of 65536 bytes, with the same physical count.

For a 512-byte block size, those examples represent 32768 and
65536 bytes respectively.  0x4D000040 is NOT 1291845696 blocks.
It is an encoded field.  Do not enter it into the GUI block gadget.

The library decodes tagged factors for use; its live
ix_fs_buf_factor retains the ordinary product (blocks * multiplier).
An untagged factor is interpreted as a plain physical block count
with multiplier 1.  ix_get_settings() returns a tagged 80.3 record
so the three settings can be preserved when saved again.

A preliminary, untagged 80.3 draft represented a 64 * 2 setting
as the plain factor 128.  That value cannot reveal whether it once
meant 128 * 1 or 64 * 2.  The current reader interprets it as 128
physical blocks at 1x, preserving the same effective I/O size.

IMPORTANT: This describes the tagged encoding implemented in the
15 November 2026 sources.  A different proposed encoding designed
to be readable by 80.2 has NOT been implemented.  Do not confuse
that proposal with the current on-disk format.
@endnode

@node compat803 "Compatibility with ixemul 80.2 and earlier"
Direction matters:

  80.2 preferences -> ixemul 80.3
    Ordinary positive fs_buf_factor values are recognized as the
    physical block count at 1x.  The normal old red-zone value of
    zero maps to the default 512-byte block size.  On saving through
    the updated ixprefs, the output uses the tagged 80.3 format.

  80.3 tagged preferences -> ixemul 80.2
    UNSAFE.  The old library does not know the marker and may read
    0x4D000040 as the decimal integer 1291845696 physical blocks.
    This can produce grossly incorrect sizes or overflow arithmetic.
    Do NOT use a tagged 80.3 file unchanged after downgrading.

  80.3 tagged preferences -> ixprefscheck --to-legacy
    The converter produces a new revision-80.2 record.  It requires
    a block size of 512 bytes, since the old format has no field
    for a new block size.  For multiplier 2x it folds the multiplier
    into the plain physical count to retain total I/O bytes.

Example: 512 * 64 * 2 = 65536 is exported as 512 * 128 * 1.
This preserves the effective size but loses the separate 64/2x
component distinction when read again by older software.

Very old records with nonzero historical red_zone_size deserve
extra review.  The current ix_settings.c uses a positive value in
that slot as a block size; ixprefscheck interprets the obsolete slot
as historical data when the record is labelled older than 80.3.

A preference file is a binary record, not a text file.  Its exact
length of 28 bytes does not guarantee that the settings are valid.
Check its contents with @{"ixprefscheck" link check803}.
@endnode

@node check803 "ixprefscheck 1.0: separate CLI utility"
ixprefscheck is NOT the built-in ixprefs command-line interface.
It reads a named ixemul.prefs file, displays its raw and interpreted
values, and optionally creates a converted COPY under a new name.
It does not invoke ix_get_settings() or ix_set_settings().

ixprefscheck is built as part of the normal ixemul utils build.

Usage:

  ixprefscheck <preferences-file>
  ixprefscheck --convert [--zero-membuf] <input> <new-output>
  ixprefscheck --to-legacy [--zero-membuf] <input> <new-output>
  ixprefscheck --help

Inspect only:

  ixprefscheck ENV:ixemul.prefs
  ixprefscheck ENVARC:ixemul.prefs
  ixprefscheck RAM:backup.prefs

Output includes file version/revision, encoding, decoded flags,
profiling mode, MEMBUF, block size, physical blocks, multiplier,
total preferred I/O size, stored factor, network type and status.
Inspection only reads the input file and does not update ENV:,
ENVARC: or live ixemul settings.

Conversion creates a NEW file with open(O_CREAT|O_EXCL).  If the
target already exists, the command fails instead of overwriting it.
The input file is never modified.  Do not direct the output to an
existing ENV:ixemul.prefs and expect an in-place update.

The utility uses ordinary C/POSIX I/O, so its executable may depend
on the normal GCC C runtime even though it uses no ixemul settings
API.  It is designed for C89/GCC 2.95.3; native runtime behaviour
must still be tested on the target system.

For exact acceptance rules, see @{"Validation" link validation803}.
For migration examples, see @{"Conversion" link convert803}.
@endnode

@node validation803 "ixprefscheck validation and result codes"
ixprefscheck performs the following checks when reading a file:

 * It must be exactly 28 bytes (not shorter or longer).
 * Version is in the supported range 47..80, revision nonnegative.
   A version 80 record with revision above 3 is rejected.
 * Unknown flag bits are rejected; profiling mode 3 is warned about.
 * Network type must be 0..3 (Auto, None, AS225, AmiTCP).
 * Block size, physical count and multiplier must be valid, with the
   combined I/O size no more than 524288 bytes (512 KiB).
 * A negative MEMBUF value is rejected.  A positive MEMBUF value
   produces a warning because it is unreliable in tested 80.3 builds.
 * A tagged factor in a legacy-labelled record produces a warning.
 * An untagged revision 80.3 factor is treated as physical blocks
   at 1x, with a warning about the ambiguous earlier draft.

The utility reports these result classes:

  RESULT: OK.         No detected errors or warnings
  RESULT: WARNINGS.   Review the listed cautions
  RESULT: INVALID.    File is rejected for conversion

Exit status in inspection mode:

   0  Settings passed all checks
   5  Valid but warnings were issued
  10  Error (including invalid record or file I/O error)
  20  Incorrect command-line usage

A successful conversion returns 0 after writing its new file, even
if earlier diagnostics reported a warning that was resolved or
accepted for conversion.  A conversion never proceeds if validation
found an ERROR.

These checks are more diagnostic than ix_set_settings() alone.  The
library's block-limit guard makes its use of I/O factors safer, but
it does not comprehensively reject every unknown flag or positive
MEMBUF value.  The checker works on raw file data, so it can show
problems that the library might silently default or ignore.
@endnode

@node convert803 "Converting preferences: worked examples"
TO 80.3 TAGGED FORMAT:

  ixprefscheck --convert old.prefs RAM:converted.prefs

The new record is stamped 80.3.  The physical count and 1x or 2x
multiplier are stored in the tagged factor, and the block size is
stored in the former red-zone field.  A normal 80.2 file with factor
64 produces 512 bytes, 64 blocks, 1x, total 32768 bytes.

TO 80.2-READABLE LEGACY FORMAT:

  ixprefscheck --to-legacy new.prefs RAM:legacy.prefs

The new record is stamped 80.2, uses the plain factor, and sets the
old red-zone field to zero.  Conversion fails if the source block
size is not 512 bytes.  If the source uses 2x, the converter stores
physical_blocks * 2 as the plain legacy factor.

TO ZERO A POSITIVE MEMBUF LIMIT:

  ixprefscheck --convert --zero-membuf old.prefs RAM:safe.prefs

The source file is unchanged; only RAM:safe.prefs has MEMBUF 0.
Without --zero-membuf, conversion of a positive MEMBUF value is
refused.  This is deliberate, not a silent modification.

SAFE PROCEDURE:

  1. Make a backup of every existing preferences file.
  2. Inspect the source with ixprefscheck (no conversion flags).
  3. Convert to a NEW destination name, preferably on RAM:.
  4. Inspect the new output and check the decoded settings.
  5. Only after verification, install the chosen file into ENV:
     and, if permanent settings are wanted, ENVARC:.
  6. Restart or reload the library when needed to verify startup
     behaviour; do not assume its already-loaded settings changed.

The checker itself never installs converted preferences implicitly.
Do not confuse its --convert option with ixprefs Save or Use.
@endnode

@node troubles803 "Troubleshooting settings, files and handlers"
PREFERENCES FILE NOT FOUND:
  ixemul's intended fallback when GetVar() cannot read a full
  settings record is to apply defaults.  If a GUI crashes only
  without ixemul.prefs, investigate initialization and the runtime;
  do not assume that creating a dummy file is the correct repair.

ENVARC: OR ENV: NOT ASSIGNED:
  Check the actual assignments and storage paths before Save or
  Last Saved.  A system using an ENV: handler need not keep a copy
  of persistent preferences in RAM:ENV.  Assignment behaviour can
  differ among AmigaOS-compatible systems.

IXPREFS REPORTS EIO ON SAVE OR USE:
  Some filesystem handlers have been observed to truncate a file
  successfully while returning a non-EOF value from SetFileSize().
  Tests on an ENV: handler showed a nonzero result even after a
  successful truncate-to-zero.  This is an observed deviation,
  NOT a documented rule that the result is the previous file size.
  The ftruncate() correction proposed for ixemul 80.3 checks the
  actual EOF while preserving POSIX file position; installation and
  regression testing must confirm the library build used.

HUGE BLOCK COUNT DISPLAYED AFTER A DOWNGRADE:
  A tagged 80.3 file may have been read by 80.2.  For example,
  0x4D000040 is a tag for 64 blocks at 1x, not a count of more
  than a billion blocks.  Do not save or use that value under 80.2.
  Convert the file with --to-legacy and inspect the new copy.

MEMBUF GREATER THAN ZERO:
  Leave it at 0 in current tested 80.3 builds; positive values have
  caused configure failures.  See @{"MEMBUF" link membuf}.

INVALID BLOCK COMBINATION:
  The maximum preferred I/O size is 512 KiB.  Reduce the block
  size, physical count or multiplier; see @{"I/O settings" link io_settings}.

GUI CRASH AT STARTUP:
  A separate issue from truncation during Save/Use.  Compare GUI
  and CLI operation, the state of ENV:, library version and stack.
  The cause must be diagnosed separately; neither ixprefscheck nor
  the ftruncate change should be represented as a proven GUI fix.
@endnode
@node installation "Installation and upgrading"
Copy ixprefs and, optionally, ixprefs.info to a suitable directory.
The updated executable should be paired with ixemul.library 80.3
for its new block-size and multiplier controls to be meaningful.

The Makefile in the ixemul utils directory builds ixprefscheck.
Copy the resulting executable to a directory in your command search
path if desired.

IMPORTANT: Before upgrading or downgrading, make backup copies of
ENV:ixemul.prefs and ENVARC:ixemul.prefs wherever those files exist.
An 80.3 tagged preference file is NOT safe for ixemul 80.2.
Use @{"ixprefscheck" link check803} to inspect and export one if needed.
Do not overwrite an original file while testing conversion.

On classic AmigaOS, ENV: contains active environment variables and
ENVARC: stores persistent values.  Another operating system or an
ENV: handler may implement these paths differently.  Ensure that
both assignments work before relying on Save or Last Saved.

@endnode
@node recompiling "Recompiling"
Ixprefs is now integrated into the ixemul.library source distribution.

The AmigaOS GUI has been designed using GadToolsBox 2.0c by Jan van
den Baard.  The makefile patches the C code generated by GadToolsBox
(amiga_gui.c) using a sed script, to add support for AmigaOS 3.x features.
This script should work for other programs generated by GadToolsBox,
so you might want to use it in other projects as well.

When creating C source code from within GadToolsBox, you will create
files amiga_gui.c and amiga_gui_temp.c.  The latter contains templates
for all functions called to handle gadgets and menus.  If you have not
added any new gadgets or menus, you can discard this file, otherwise cut
the templates for the new functions and paste them to amiga_functions.c.

80.3 development note (November 2026): the new gadgets were edited in
the C source.  The old binary GadToolsBox project (amiga_gui.gui) was
NOT updated.  Regenerating amiga_gui.c from that unmodified .gui file
would lose the new controls.  Synchronize the GadToolsBox project
before regenerating source from it.
@endnode
@node window "The ixprefs window (80.3)"
@font topaz.font 11
 @{"   " link amigawildcard} Amiga wildcards
 @{"   " link case} Case sensitive pattern matching
 @{"   " link slash} Translate / (advanced option)
 @{"   " link noflush} Do not flush library
 @{"   " link showstack} Show stack usage
 @{"   " link ignoreenv} Ignore global environment (ENV:)
 @{"   " link suppress} Suppress the "Insert volume in drive" requester

 @{"      " link blocksize} Block size (bytes)
 @{"      " link blocks} Physical blocks build one logical block
 @{"      " link iomultiplier} Extra I/O multiplier (1x or 2x)
 @{"      " link membuf} Membuf size
 @{" Reported block size " link reportedblock} (read-only diagnostic)

 @{"     <type>     " link networking} Network support
 @{"     <type>     " link profiling} Profiling method
 @{"   " link enforcer} Create Enforcer hit on trap
 @{"   " link mufs} Enable MuFS support
 @{"   " link advanced} Enable advanced ixprefs options

 @{" Save " link save}   @{" Use " link use}   @{" Cancel " link cancel}

The I/O controls are independent.  The three values determine a
preferred logical I/O size, not the physical layout of the disk.
See @{"I/O settings and examples" link io_settings}.
@endnode
@node amigawildcard "Allow AmigaOS-style wildcards"
The ixemul.library startup code expands wildcards in the command line.  If
this option is set, AmigaOS-style wildcards are recognized, in addition to
UNIX-style wildcards which are always recognized.  Thus, if this option is
set, you can use either *.c or #?.c to specify all C source files, while if
this option is not set, you can only use *.c.
@endnode
@node case "Case sensitive pattern matching"
If this option is set, pattern matching in UNIX-style wildcard expansion is
case sensitive, i.e., *.c expands to all files ending in .c (lower case), while
*.C expands to all files ending in .C (upper case).  If this option is not
set, UNIX-style wildcard expansion is case insensitive (e.g., *.c expands to
files ending in both .c and .C).
@endnode
@node slash "Translate /"
If this option is set, ixemul.library interprets "/foo" as "foo:", and
"foo//bar" as "foo/bar".

Note: this option is only available on the ixprefs graphical interface if
the @{"Enable advanced ixprefs options" link advanced} checkbox is checked.
@endnode
@node noflush "Do not flush library"
If this option is set, then ixemul.library will always remain resident in
memory, and will not be flushed when no programs are using it.  This can be
used to ensure that the library settings are not lost by flushing and then
reloading the library, at the cost of making the memory occupied by the
library unavailable to other programs.  It is also slightly faster, because
the library is loaded only once.

When this option is not set, then it will be possible to flush ixemul.library
when no programs use it (e.g., by typing "avail flush" at the CLI prompt or by
selecting the Debug->flushlibs item from the workbench menu).
@endnode
@node showstack "Show stack usage"
When this option is set, whenever a program exits, ixemul.library sends a
message to the "ixstack port" port, reporting the stack usage of the program
that just exited. When run with the -s option, the @{"ixstack" Link "GG:man/man1/ixstack.1/Main"} program will
monitor these messages and print the stack usage and the total stack size of
each program that exits.

When this option is not set, no such messages are sent.

You can use this option to estimate the stack requirements of a program, and
then use @{"ixstack" Link "GG:man/man1/ixstack.1/Main"} to set the program's minimum stack size.
@endnode
@node ignoreenv "Ignore global environment"
If this option is set, the getenv() function returns local environment
variables, i.e., those created by the AmigaOS "set" command".  If this
option is not set, the getenv() function returns global environment variables,
i.e., those created by the AmigaOS "setenv" command", in addition to local
ones.  Local environment variables have precedence over global ones.
@endnode
@node enforcer "Generate Enforcer hit when a trap occurs"
If this option is set then, whenever a trap (division by zero, bus error,
etc.) occurs, ixemul.library writes the error code to the address 0xdeaddead.
Using the stack dump from Enforcer you can then try to find out where the
problem occurred.  If this option is not set, no Enforcer hit is generated.

Note: this option is only available on the ixprefs graphical interface if
the @{"Enable advanced ixprefs options" link advanced} checkbox is checked.
@endnode
@node suppress "Suppress the `Insert volume in drive' requester"
If this option is not set, whenever ixemul.library tries to access a
non-existent volume, it will pop up a requester asking for the volume to be
inserted.  If this option is set, ixemul.library will not pop up this
requester.
@endnode
@node membuf "Membuf size - current restrictions"
MEMBUF is the maximum file size eligible for ixemul's memory-backed
read-only file path.  A value of zero disables that optimization.

IMPORTANT FOR IXEMUL 80.3:
Nonzero MEMBUF settings have caused problems, including configure
failures, in tested 80.3 builds.  The currently recommended and
reliably tested setting is 0.  Do not increase MEMBUF merely because
more RAM is available; a positive value is not presently supported
reliably in this project.

ixprefs 80.3 still exposes the setting.  ix_set_settings() currently
accepts nonnegative MEMBUF values; its I/O-buffer limit does NOT
validate the safety of this separate feature.  A negative MEMBUF is
ignored by the library rather than applied.

@{"ixprefscheck" link check803} reports MEMBUF = 0 as recommended,
warns for positive values, and rejects negative values.  Converting
positive MEMBUF values requires --zero-membuf, which changes only the
new output copy, not the input file or running library.
@endnode
@node networking "Networking support"
This option allows the user to specify the kind of networking support they
have:

0. Auto detect (default): ixemul tries to figure out what networking support
   the user has (none, AmiTCP, AS225).
   
1. No networking: all networking is turned off, regardless of whether the user
   has AmiTCP or AS225 or not.

2. AS225: set to AS225, if available.  Otherwise no network support.

3. AmiTCP: set to AmiTCP, if available.  Otherwise no network support.

Note that `Auto detect' will check for AmiTCP first, and AS225 second.  This
is to circumvent problems when the socket.library emulation for AmiTCP is
used.
@endnode
@node mufs "Enable MuFS support"
If this option is set, then ixemul.library will enable special support for
the Multiuser Filesystem (MuFS) if the multiuser.library is present. Not
everybody wants this functionality, and since it turns out that the
support is actually somewhat buggy, ixemul.library no longer supports
MuFS by default, so you will have to turn this checkbox on if you want
it. This checkbox is unique compared to the others, in that it won't be
in effect until the ixemul.library has been flushed from memory.
@endnode
@node profiling "Profiling method"
Gcc provides the -pg flag to enable code profiling.  When a program is
compiled with -pg, a special function is called at the beginning of each
function, which registers 1) how many times this function is called, 2)
which functions called this function.

In addition to this, a function in ixemul.library is called 50 times per
second.  This function, among other things, increases timers and also,
if the current program is compiled with -pg, samples the current program
counter (PC) and registers this.

When a program that is compiled with the -pg option has finished, a file
called gmon.out is produced, containing the above two sets of statistics.
Using the gprof program, you can read this file and determine which
programs were called and how much time was spent in each function.
The timing information is derived from the PC sampling.

The problem with this scheme is that if the PC is outside the code in your
program, nothing is registered.  Suppose a function calls a ROM function
or some library function that takes 10 seconds to finish.  According to
gprof, this function took 0 seconds, because the time spent elsewhere
is not counted.  This may be what you want (no ``pollution'' from other
sources), but there are cases where this is not what is needed.

Thus, ixemul.library provides three methods of sampling the PC:
1. Only in program (default): take samples only while your program is
   running and the PC is within your program.
2. Only in task: take samples while your program is running and if the PC is
   outside your program code (e.g. in ixemul or in intuition.library), use
   the last function your program entered before calling ixemul or whatever.
3. Profile always: always take samples, even if other programs are running.
   Again, the last recorded function gets the hit in those cases.

The choice of option depends on what you are interested in profiling: the
performance of just your own code (in UNIX terms, the ``user time''),
the overall performance of your program (in UNIX terms, ``user'' plus
``system'' time), or the performance of your program plus any external
programs that your program invokes (e.g., using the system() function).
@endnode
@node blocks "Physical blocks per logical I/O block"
This editable number is the count of physical blocks combined into
one preferred logical I/O block.  It is separate from both the size
of each block and the I/O multiplier.

For example, 64 blocks of 512 bytes with a multiplier of 1x gives
64 * 512 * 1 = 32768 bytes (32 KiB).  With 2x it gives 64 KiB.

Valid physical-block counts are at least 1 and at most 65535, but
the @{"512 KiB total I/O limit" link io_settings} normally imposes a
smaller maximum for a particular block-size/multiplier combination.

The CLI option is -b N or --fs-buf-factor N.  Despite its historical
name, -b now sets the physical block count, not the complete encoded
80.3 value.  Do NOT enter the tagged number 0x4D000040 in this field.
@endnode
@node advanced "Enable advanced ixprefs options"
When this checkbox is selected, the @{"Translate /" link slash} and @{"Create Enforcer hit on trap" link enforcer}
options become available on the ixprefs window.  This checkbox is unchecked
by default.
@endnode
@node save "Save"
Applies the selected configuration to the running ixemul.library,
then writes ENV:ixemul.prefs and ENVARC:ixemul.prefs and exits on
success.  The stored format is the 80.3 tagged 28-byte record.

The new I/O controls are checked against the 512 KiB maximum before
Save proceeds.  Save stops if Use fails to write ENV:.  A write error
is reported rather than silently ignored.

Note: The implementation applies the settings to the live library
before it opens ENV:ixemul.prefs.  Thus, a write failure can leave
live settings changed even though the file was not saved.  Back up
existing preferences before experimenting with new settings.
@endnode
@node use "Use"
Applies the configuration to the running ixemul.library, writes
ENV:ixemul.prefs, and exits on success.  It does not intentionally
write ENVARC:ixemul.prefs; changes may be lost at the next boot.

The selected I/O settings must satisfy the 512 KiB maximum.
If writing ENV: fails, a requester or CLI error is reported, but
settings may already have been applied in memory.
@endnode
@node cancel "Cancel"
Exits the program without making any changes to the configuration.
@endnode
@node menu "The ixprefs menu"
 Project  Edit
 @{"Save " link save}    @{"Reset to defaults" link defaults}
 @{"Use  " link use}    @{"Last saved       " link last}
 @{"About" link about}    @{"Restore          " link restore}
 @{"Quit " link quit}
@endnode
@node CLI "CLI usage"
Type "ixprefs" without any arguments, to get the @{"ixprefs window" link window}.

Alternatively, you can control the program from the CLI.  You can use either
short options, e.g., "-m 1024", or long options, e.g., "--membuf-limit 1024".
If you use long options, you need only specify enough of the option name to
disambiguate it from other option names, e.g., "--mem 1024".

The following CLI options are available:

-a 1, --allow-amiga-wildcard
  @{"accept AmigaOS wildcard notation" link amigawildcard}
-a 0, --no-allow-amiga-wildcard
  @{"do not accept AmigaOS wildcard notation" link amigawildcard}
-s 1, --unix-pattern-matching-case-sensitive
  @{"use case sensitive UNIX pattern matching" link case}
-s 0, --no-unix-pattern-matching-case-sensitive
  do not @{"use case sensitive UNIX patter matching" link case}
-/ 1, --translate-slash
  @{"translate /foo -> foo: and a//b -> a/b" link slash}
-/ 0, --no-translate-slash
  do not @{"translate /foo -> foo: and a//b -> a/b" link slash}
-m N, --membuf-limit N
  @{"MEMBUF maximum in bytes; keep 0 in current 80.3 builds" link membuf}
-b N, --fs-buf-factor N
  @{"N physical blocks per logical I/O block" link blocks}
-B N, --block-size N
  @{"filesystem block size in bytes (default 512)" link blocksize}
-X N, --io-multiplier N
  @{"I/O buffer multiplier: N is 1 or 2" link iomultiplier}
  The three values must total no more than 512 KiB.
-i 1, --ignore-global-env
  @{"ignore global environment" link ignoreenv} (ENV:)
-i 0, --no-ignore-global-env
  @{"do not ignore global environment" link ignoreenv} (ENV:)
-e 1, --enforcer-hit
  @{"generate Enforcer hit" link enforcer} when a trap occurs
-e 0, --no-enforcer-hit
  @{"do not generate Enforcer hit" link enforcer} when a trap occurs
-v 0, --insert-disk-requester
  @{"suppress the `Insert volume in drive' requester" link suppress}
-v 1, --noinsert-disk-requester
  @{"do not suppress the `Insert volume in drive' requester" link suppress}
-u 0, --no-stack-usage
  @{"do not show the stack usage" link showstack}
-u 1, --stack-usage
  @{"show the stack usage" link showstack}
-f 0, --flush-library
  @{"prevent ixemul.library from being flushed from memory" link noflush}
-f 1, --no-flush-library
  @{"do not prevent ixemul.library from being flushed from memory" link noflush}
-n 0, --auto-detect
  set @{"networking support" link networking} to auto detect
-n 1, --no-networking
  turn off @{"networking support" link networking}
-n 2, --as225
  use AS225 @{"networking support" link networking}
-n 3, --amitcp
  use AmiTCP @{"networking support" link networking}
-p 0, --profile program
  @{"profile" link profiling} the program only
-p 1, --profile-task
  @{"profile" link profiling} while the task is running
-p 2, --profile-always
  always @{"profile" link profiling} your program
-d, --default
  reset settings to defaults (other options are ignored)
-L, --last-saved
  reset settings from ENVARC:ixemul.prefs
  (other options, including --default, are ignored)
-M 1, --support-mufs
  @{"enable MuFS support" link mufs}
-M 0, --no-support-mufs
  @{"do not enable MuFS support" link mufs}
-S, --save
  save new configuration
-R, --report
  display configuration; see the note below on side effects
-V, --version
  displays information about the @{"program" link distribution} and its @{"author" link author}
  (other options, including --default and --last-saved are ignored)
-h, --help
  display the various ixprefs options
80.3 examples:

  ixprefs -b 64 -B 512 -X 1 -R
  ixprefs -b 64 -B 512 -X 2 -S
  ixprefs -b 64 -B 4096 -X 2 -S

The first setting is 32768 bytes; the second is 65536 bytes;
the third is the maximum allowed 524288 bytes.  For instance,
-b 65 -B 4096 -X 2 exceeds the limit and is rejected.

WARNING: The current ixprefs CLI executes Use() for ordinary calls
without --save, even when --report (-R) is present.  Such a call
may change live settings and write ENV:ixemul.prefs.  Use the
read-only @{"ixprefscheck" link check803} to inspect an existing
preferences file without changing anything.  --help and --version
are special informational cases and do not call Use().
@endnode
@node about "About"
Displays information about the @{"program" link distribution} and its @{"author" link author}.
@endnode
@node quit "Quit"
Exits the program without making any changes to the configuration.
@endnode
@node defaults "Reset to defaults"
Resets the various options to their default values.
@endnode
@node last "Last saved"
Reads the configuration stored in ENVARC:ixemul.prefs.
@endnode
@node restore "Restore"
Sets the various options to the values that are currently being used by
ixemul.library.
@endnode
@node history "Program history"
Version 1.0	June 23, 1995
First public release.

Version 1.1	June 26, 1995
* Made the following enhancements, suggested by Kamil Iskra
  (iskra@student.uci.agh.edu.pl):
  + Ixprefs window is now font adaptive.
  + Ixprefs window is now activated when opened.
  + GZZ adjust flag in GadToolsBox turned off.
  + CheckBox gadgets are now scalable under AmigaOS 3.x.
  + NewLook menus under AmigaOS 3.x
  + Use OpenFont() rather than DiskFont() to access system font, as this font
    is always in memory.
  + Slightly rearranged window layout to avoid problems with sizable gadgets.
* Updated the documentation.

Version 1.2	July 3, 1995
* Added a UNIX-style CLI interface using getopt_long().
* Modified showrequester() to print messages on stderr if the ixprefs window
  is not open, so that no requesters pop up when the CLI interface is used.
* Updated ixemul.library version check, so that the program now runs under
  both version 41.0 and 41.1 without producing a warning.
* Fixed the dates in this section.  Not that anyone cares, but the previous
  versions of this program were written in June, not May!
* Updated the documentation.

Version 1.3	(not released)
* Made a very minor modification in gadget placement (some gadgets were off
  by one pixel).
* Fixed a bug where if AmigaOS notation is turned off, it was impossible to
  load or save the configuration file.  Remaining bug: if neither AmigaOS
  notation nor / translation is allowed, there is no way to access S:ixprefs,
  and loading/saving is impossible.  This could be fixed by temporarily
  twiddling the flags.  However, as the "allow AmigaOS notation" flag has been
  made obsolete in version 41.3, I don't plan to fix this.
* Modified Makefile to remove ixprefs.c.orig.

Version 2.0
		beta 1, July 12, 1995
* Made the following modifications in preparation for the integration of
  ixprefs with the forthcoming cleaned-up version of ixemul.library:
  + Dropped support for ix_unix_pattern_matching, ix_no_ces_then_open_console,
    ix_ignore_global_env, ix_translate_dots, ix_force_translation, and
    ix_translate_symlinks.  These settings will no longer be honored by
    ixemul.library, as their values will be fixed.
  + Added support for the new ix_allow_amiga_wildcard setting.
  + Rearranged the GUI accordingly.
  + Applied Kamil Iskra's modifications for AmigaOS 3.x to the resulting
    ixprefs.c, and created a new ixprefs.c.diff.
  + Changed the save format from human-readable to machine-readable (struct
    settings).  I know many people will disagree with this decision, but it
    was necessary for easier parsing of the configuration by ixemul.library.
  + Added struct settings to ixemul.h.
  + Modified the definition of the ixemul flags in ixemul.h from a bitfield to
    a long word, with a set of #define's for the various flags.
  + Changed the location of the save file from S:ixprefs to ENVARC:IXPREFS.
    The "save" and "use" options also save the configuration in ENV:IXPREFS.
* Modified main.c to call ixprefsrestore() to load the ixprefs settings from
  ixemulbase instead of duplicating ixprefsrestore()'s code.
* Updated the documentation.
		beta 2, September 16, 1995
* Saved configuration now includes values for obsolete flags, so that ixprefs
  may work with earlier versions of ixemul.library.
* Cleaned up the code by compiling with -Wall and eliminating the warnings.
* Changed the name of the configuration file from IXPREFS to ixconfig.prefs,
  to avoid a clash with the ixprefs executable in the unlikely case that
  someone might have ENV: or ENVARC: in their path.
* Fixed a typo ("upto" instead of "up to") in the text printed by the --help
  option.
* Added a version string to main.c.
* Added support for the new ix_do_not_flush_library setting.
* Applied Kamil Iskra's modifications for AmigaOS 3.x to the new version of
  ixprefs.c, and created a new ixprefs.c.diff.
* Updated the documentation.
		beta 3, October 25, 1995
* Corrected the punctuation of the configuration report in CLI mode.
* Updated private copy of ixemul.h to reflect the changes in the official
  version (41.4).
* Added two paragraphs that were missing from the GPL notice in the source
  files, Makefile, and documentation.
* Changed the method of modifying ixprefs.c to add support for AmigaOS 3.x
  features: instead of applying a diff file to ixprefs.c (clumsy, as the diff
  file does not work if the GUI is changed) I now use a sed script that
  modifies ixprefs.c on the fly (this should work even if the GUI changes, and
  should work for other projects, too).  Kept a copy of ixprefs.c.diff around,
  just in case...
* Recompiled using -fbaserel.  The resulting executable is almost 2K smaller.
* Added back support for ix_ignore_global_env.  Support for this option will
  not be dropped from future versions of ixemul.library.
* Made minor modifications in the help text in cli.c (s/don't/do not/).
* Updated the documentation.
		beta 4, October 25, 1995 (not distributed)
* ix_do_not_flush_library and ix_ignore_global_env were not being restored
  when doing a "last saved".

Version 2.1	November 14, 1995
Adapted for ixemul.library 42.0 by Hans Verkuil:
* Independently applied a similar fix to that of version 2.0 beta 4.
* Finished merging ixprefs into the main distribution.
* Changed several things because all library base changes (such as the config
  settings) now go through library calls.
* Removed another setting (fibcache, was never used).
* Added a new cycle gadget for future networking support.  The library accepts
  it, but currently ignores it.
* Rearranged the gadgets in the GUI, and changed the wording in the ixprefs
  window and the output of the --report option, for a more aesthetically
  pleasing appearance.
* Updated the version check in main.c to check for version 42 of
  ixemul.library, as ixprefs is now incompatible with version 41.0.
* As ixprefs is now incompatible with versions prior to 42.0, ixprefs no
  longer stores default values for obsolete flags.
* Switched back to compiling without -fbaserel, as we had a manifestation of
  the infamous "spilled register" problem.

* Threw away ixprefs.c.diff, as the sed script proved capable of handling
  the massive rearrangement of the GUI that occurred.
* Removed the various tooltypes from ixprefs.info, which I had forgotten to
  delete when copying the icon from sys:prefs.
* Updated the documentation.

Version 2.2	March 17, 1996
* Adapted for ixemul.library 43.0 by Hans Verkuil (added support for
  ix_create_enforcer_hit).
* Minor cleanup (updated version to 2.2).
* Updated the documentation.

Version 2.3	August 6, 1996
Adapted for ixemul.library 44.0 by Hans Verkuil:
* Added support for ix_profile_method_mask.
* Updated the version check in main.c to check for version 44 of
  ixemul.library, as ixprefs is now incompatible with version 43.0.

* Minor cleanup (updated version number, Copyright date, author's e-mail
  address).
* Updated the documentation.

Version 2.4	September 23, 1996
Adapted for ixemul.library 45.0 by Hans Verkuil:
* Removed stack watcher and red zone.
* Updated the documentation.

Version 2.5	June 14, 1997
Adapted for ixemul.library 47.0 by Hans Verkuil:
* Added MuFS support checkbox.
* Updated the documentation.

Version 2.6	June 21, 1997
* Massive code rearrangement to support more than one operating system.
* Updated the documentation.

Version 2.7	August 15, 1997
* Added "Enable advanced ixprefs options" checkbox, to selectively
  enable/disable some "dangerous" ixemul opytions from the GUI.

Version 2.8	April 24, 2001
* Changed the text of the "Check stack usage" button to
  "Show stack usage", to agree with the command line version of the
  option and the actual function of the option.
* Added the desription of the "Show stack usage option" in the
  documentation.

ixemul 80.3 / ixprefs source update  November 14-16, 2026
  ChatGPT modifications (JJ)
* Extended the GUI and CLI with adjustable block size, physical block
  count, and a 1x/2x I/O multiplier, subject to a 512 KiB total limit.
* Kept the 28-byte settings structure but added tagged factor encoding.
* Retained legacy factor decoding and documented downgrade hazards.
* Added a reported-volume-block-size diagnostic button.
* Documented the currently unreliable MEMBUF > 0 configuration.
* Added ixprefscheck 1.0 for separate inspection and conversion.
* Extended this guide while retaining the historical release history.
* This is a source update for library 80.3; the application still
  identifies itself as ixprefs v2.8.

